{
  "title": "Retire API versions before they pile up",
  "content": "Opinions on api versioning tend to change fast after watching it fail somewhere it wasn't supposed to. This post covers what comes after the happy path. The vendor docs cover the setup and go quiet on the failure modes.\n\n## Unpopular opinion: most API versioning is just procrastination\n\nTeams tend to treat version numbers as a kind of insurance policy. You ship `/v1/`, then one day a breaking change is needed, so you ship `/v2/`, and the old one \"stays around for compatibility.\" Six months later you have `/v1/`, `/v2/`, and a half-finished `/v3/` that only two internal services call. The graveyard isn't caused by versioning itself — it's caused by versioning *without a deletion plan*. A version that has no scheduled death is not a compatibility strategy, it's a backlog item you'll never pick up.\n\nThis post is about versioning strategies that come with an exit built in. Not \"how to design a REST API\" — you already know that. The focus is on the mechanics that make old versions cheap to retire, and the specific points where teams accidentally make retirement impossible.\n\nBy the end you'll have a working example in FastAPI showing a version negotiation pattern, a deprecation header scheme, and a migration checklist you can run against an existing API in an afternoon.\n\n## The three versioning strategies, and which one actually survives\n\nThere are three mainstream approaches. They are not equivalent in how much they cost you later.\n\n| Strategy | Where the version lives | Cost to add a version | Cost to remove a version |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| URI versioning | `/v1/resource` | Low | High — every client URL changes |\n| Header versioning | `Accept: application/vnd.api+json;version=1` | Medium | Medium — clients must send a new header |\n| Date-based versioning | `Stripe-Version: 2024-06-20` | High | Low — you can pin behavior per request |\n\nURI versioning is the default choice because it's visible and cacheable. The trap is that it makes the version part of the *address* rather than part of the *contract*. When you want to retire `/v1/`, you have to convince every consumer to change a URL, which means coordinating with clients you may not control. That's where graveyards come from.\n\nHeader versioning keeps the URL stable, which means you can route by header at the gateway without touching client URLs. The cost is that clients that don't set the header need a default, and that default becomes its own implicit version you have to support.\n\nDate-based versioning, as used by Stripe, is the most expensive to implement and the easiest to retire. Each request declares the API behavior it expects, so you can keep old behavior alive for old dates while new clients get new behavior on the same URL. The implementation cost is real: every response shape change needs to be conditional on the request's declared date.\n\nIf you're starting fresh and you control all clients, URI versioning plus a strict deprecation policy is fine. If you have external clients you can't force to upgrade, date-based versioning is the only one that lets you move forward without a negotiation.\n\n## Step 1 — Set up the environment\n\nYou need Python 3.10+ and FastAPI. The example uses only standard library and FastAPI dependencies.\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"fastapi[standard]\"\n```\n\nCreate a file `main.py`. The pattern below uses a single application with a version resolver that reads from either the URL path or a header, so you can migrate incrementally.\n\n## Step 2 — Core implementation: version resolution as a dependency\n\nThe mistake teams make is scattering version checks through route handlers. If `if version == 1:` appears in a handler, you've already lost — the version logic is now coupled to business logic and you can't remove it without touching every endpoint.\n\nInstead, resolve the version once, in a dependency, and pass it to handlers as a value. Handlers decide behavior based on the value, not on parsing.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Header, HTTPException, Depends, Request\nfrom typing import Annotated\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nSUPPORTED_VERSIONS = {\"1\", \"2\"}\nDEFAULT_VERSION = \"2\"\nDEPRECATED_VERSIONS = {\"1\"}\n\nasync def resolve_version(\n    request: Request,\n    x_api_version: Annotated[str | None, Header()] = None,\n) -> str:\n    # Prefer explicit header, fall back to path segment, then default.\n    explicit = x_api_version\n    if explicit is None:\n        path_parts = request.url.path.strip(\"/\").split(\"/\")\n        if path_parts and path_parts[0].startswith(\"v\") and path_parts[0][1:].isdigit():\n            explicit = path_parts[0][1:]\n    version = explicit or DEFAULT_VERSION\n    if version not in SUPPORTED_VERSIONS:\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=400,\n            detail=f\"Unsupported API version: {version}\",\n        )\n    return version\n\nVersion = Annotated[str, Depends(resolve_version)]\n```\n\nTwo things to notice. First, the resolver is the single place that knows how to extract a version. Second, unsupported versions fail loudly at the edge, not deep inside a handler.\n\nNow a handler that behaves differently by version:\n\n```python\n@app.get(\"/v{version}/users/{user_id}\")\nasync def get_user(version: Version, user_id: int):\n    user = {\"id\": user_id, \"name\": \"Ada\", \"email\": \"ada@example.com\"}\n    if version == \"1\":\n        # v1 returned a flat `email` field. v2 nests it under contact.\n        return user\n    return {\n        \"id\": user[\"id\"],\n        \"name\": user[\"name\"],\n        \"contact\": {\"email\": user[\"email\"]},\n    }\n```\n\nThe handler's version branch is small and local. The version string itself came from the dependency, so removing v1 later means deleting one branch and one entry in `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS`.\n\n## Step 3 — Make deprecation visible and machine-readable\n\nA version you intend to remove must announce itself. The HTTP `Deprecation` header is defined in RFC 9745 (published 2025) and is the correct signal. Pair it with `Sunset` (RFC 8594) to give a date, and `Link` with `rel=\"deprecation\"` to point at migration docs.\n\nAdd a middleware that stamps every response for a deprecated version:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom fastapi import Request\nfrom starlette.middleware.base import BaseHTTPMiddleware\n\nSUNSET_DATES = {\n    \"1\": \"2026-06-01T00:00:00Z\",\n}\n\nclass DeprecationMiddleware(BaseHTTPMiddleware):\n    async def dispatch(self, request: Request, call_next):\n        response = await call_next(request)\n        version = getattr(request.state, \"api_version\", None)\n        if version in SUNSET_DATES:\n            response.headers[\"Deprecation\"] = \"true\"\n            response.headers[\"Sunset\"] = SUNSET_DATES[version]\n            response.headers[\"Link\"] = (\n                '<https://docs.example.com/migrate-v1>; rel=\"deprecation\"'\n            )\n        return response\n```\n\nTo populate `request.state.api_version`, set it inside the resolver:\n\n```python\nasync def resolve_version(request: Request, x_api_version: Annotated[str | None, Header()] = None) -> str:\n    # ... same resolution logic ...\n    request.state.api_version = version\n    return version\n```\n\nNow every v1 response carries a machine-readable warning. Clients that log headers will see it. API gateways can be configured to alert on it. This is the difference between \"we plan to remove v1\" and \"v1 is being removed on a known date.\"\n\n### The gotcha: caching proxies strip unknown headers\n\nA common trap: teams add `Deprecation` and `Sunset`, verify locally with curl, and assume clients see them. In practice, shared caches and CDNs may not forward response headers they don't recognize, and some HTTP client libraries expose only a fixed set. If you rely on the header alone to warn consumers, some fraction of them will never see it.\n\nMitigation: also include the deprecation notice in the response body for deprecated versions, and log the version on the server side so you can identify which clients are still calling v1. The header is the standard, but the log is what tells you who to contact.\n\n## Step 4 — Track usage before you announce a sunset\n\nYou cannot sunset a version you can't measure. Before publishing a sunset date, you need to know which clients call which version. The minimum viable tracking is a counter keyed by (version, client identifier, endpoint).\n\nIn FastAPI, this is a middleware or a logging hook:\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"api.version\")\n\n@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def log_version_usage(request: Request, call_next):\n    response = await call_next(request)\n    version = getattr(request.state, \"api_version\", \"unknown\")\n    client = request.headers.get(\"x-client-id\", \"anonymous\")\n    logger.info(\n        \"version_call\",\n        extra={\"version\": version, \"client\": client, \"path\": request.url.path},\n    )\n    return response\n```\n\nWhat matters is not the logging library — it's that you can answer \"which clients called v1 in the last 30 days\" before you announce anything. If you can't, the sunset date is a guess, and guesses cause outages.\n\nA reasonable policy: don't announce a sunset until v1 traffic is below a threshold you can enumerate by hand. If you can list the remaining callers on one screen, you can contact them. If you can't, you're not ready.\n\n## Step 5 — Build the migration path, not just the deprecation notice\n\nDeprecation notices tell clients something is ending. They don't tell clients what to do. The migration path is the part that actually retires versions.\n\nThree things make a migration path work:\n\n1. **A compatibility shim.** If v1 and v2 differ only in response shape, offer a query parameter or header that returns v1 shape from the v2 endpoint. This lets clients upgrade their URL without changing their parsing code, then change parsing separately. Two smaller changes are easier to schedule than one big one.\n\n2. **A changelog keyed to behavior, not to version numbers.** \"v2 changed the `email` field to `contact.email`\" is useful. \"v2 released\" is not.\n\n3. **A test fixture for each supported version.** If you can't run the old version's contract test against the current code, you don't actually support it — you're just hoping. Contract tests per version are the cheapest insurance against accidental breakage during refactors.\n\nHere's a minimal per-version contract test using FastAPI's test client:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom main import app\n\nclient = TestClient(app)\n\ndef test_v1_shape():\n    r = client.get(\"/v1/users/1\")\n    assert r.status_code == 200\n    body = r.json()\n    assert \"email\" in body\n    assert \"contact\" not in body\n    assert r.headers.get(\"Deprecation\") == \"true\"\n\ndef test_v2_shape():\n    r = client.get(\"/v2/users/1\")\n    assert r.status_code == 200\n    body = r.json()\n    assert \"contact\" in body\n    assert \"Deprecation\" not in r.headers\n```\n\nThese tests are boring and that's the point. When someone refactors the user serializer, the v1 test fails, and the question \"did we mean to break v1?\" gets asked before deploy instead of after.\n\n## Step 6 — Decide when to actually delete\n\nThe hardest part of versioning is not adding versions, it's removing them. A workable rule:\n\n- A version can be deleted when its traffic is zero for a full billing cycle, *or* when every remaining caller has been contacted and has confirmed migration.\n- The sunset date must be at least one full release cycle away from the announcement. For external APIs, that's typically measured in months, not weeks. The exact number depends on your clients' release cadence, which you should ask them about.\n- Deletion means the route returns `410 Gone`, not `404`. `404` implies the resource might exist elsewhere; `410` says it's intentionally gone. This matters for client error handling and for search engines.\n\n```python\n@app.get(\"/v1/{rest:path}\")\nasync def v1_gone(rest: str):\n    raise HTTPException(\n        status_code=410,\n        detail=\"API v1 was retired on 2026-06-01. See https://docs.example.com/migrate-v1\",\n    )\n```\n\nKeep the `410` handler in place for at least one cycle after deletion. It costs almost nothing and it turns \"my integration broke mysteriously\" into \"my integration broke with a clear message.\"\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How long should I keep a deprecated API version?**\nLong enough that every known client can migrate on their own release schedule. For internal clients that's often weeks; for external clients it can be quarters. The right way to decide is to ask the clients, not to pick a number. If you can't ask them, you probably can't safely retire the version.\n\n**Should I use URI versioning or header versioning?**\nURI versioning if you control clients and want visible, cacheable URLs. Header versioning if you need to keep URLs stable across versions. Date-based versioning if you have external clients you can't coordinate with. The cost of URI versioning is that retirement requires every client to change a URL, which is the main driver of version graveyards.\n\n**What's the difference between the Deprecation and Sunset headers?**\n`Deprecation` (RFC 9745) signals that a version is deprecated. `Sunset` (RFC 8594) gives a date after which it will stop working. Use both — deprecation without a date is a warning nobody can act on, and a date without deprecation is a surprise.\n\n**Can I just keep v1 running forever?**\nYou can, but the cost compounds. Every version you support is a version you have to test, monitor, and patch. The graveyard isn't a storage problem, it's a maintenance problem. The point of a sunset policy is to bound that cost, not to avoid it entirely.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\nVersioning strategy is a decision about deletion, not about addition. The teams that avoid graveyards are the ones that decide *before* shipping v1 what would have to be true to remove it, and then measure that condition continuously.\n\nYour next 30 minutes: open your API's route definitions and count how many versions are live. For each one, write down the date it was introduced and the last date you know a client called it. If you can't fill in the second column, that's the version you're going to have trouble retiring — and the logging middleware in Step 4 is the first thing to add.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
